Date: Fri, 19 Nov 1993 22:36:05 -0500 (EST)
From: david@bitdance.MV.COM (R. David Murray)
Subject: Oceania vs. Cyberspatial Libertaria

Nick Szabo writes:
> This is overrated.  The vast majority of our needs are still
> physical (food, housing, medicine, etc.), and while many of
> the jobs themselves involve handling information, the 
> physical plant is still there as a vulnerable bottlneck between
> the info economy and meeting our needs.

Medicine I'll give you.  (Though if Clinton's UnHealth Pogrom goes
through, we'll all probably be buying medical services on the black
market anyway.) But food and housing are a much smaller *percentage* of
the daily wage than they were even fifty years ago.  And this is a
trend that I am under the impression Extropians expect to see
accelerate.  As a society becomes more wealthy, 'basic needs' represent
a smaller and smaller percentage of daily income.  Granted, it is still
'a vast majority' now, but how much longer will that remain true?

As for x% physical workers and y% information workers . . . well, as
Duncan Frissell is fond of saying, part time temporary work and
self-employment is what the future holds for all of us, so get used to
it <grin>.  I predict that more and more people will have multiple
distinct income streams (thanks, Derek) as time goes along.  I'm
intentionally working toward that myself, but my guess is that lots of
people are being pushed into it willy-nilly.  But you are right, people
are likely to be either primarily physical workers or primarily
information workers.  However, I made a mistatement in my earlier post.
There is no need to have a physical job to pay for physical needs.  All
you need is multiple income streams, one (or more) of which is enough
to pay for your physical needs.  Those, you pay tax on.  The others,
you don't.  Because, in (proto) crypto-anarchy, how can the government even
tell you spent the money?

