Subject: Anti-Imortalism in Star Trek

Yeah, well, I could care less about the warp drive knocking holes in 
space, and that being a metaphor for chicken littlism...

What gets me about Star Trek is its use of utterly bogus, twisty,
*non-self-consistent* tech that, were it not completely tortured, would 
mean imortality for everybody. In about eight zillion different possible 
ways.

Time and again the search for immortality is shown to be ugly, wrong, 
evil, and *maladjusted.* Only *losers* want to live forever.

They have replicators popping out their asses, and they all want to die 
cuz it's so *natural.* Yeah. Right. So's smallpox and polio. I dont' see 
the federation shipping it to planets that have foolishly wiped them out.

Now, the trill on DS9 are an interesting loophole. But of course, there 
is a shortage of symbionts, so only an elite ever get to attain the Trill 
immortality. Yup, a shortage. Don't trip over the replicators! They'll 
make tea, but not PEOPLE.

Because people have SOULS. AHHHHHHH!!!!!!!!!

The Star Trek universe pisses me off. Its not the lack of capitalist 
virtues that piss me off. It is the comforting, 'oh, this is what people 
are and always will be theme.' In its own way, it broadcasts a certain 
kind of misinformation that is ultimately damaging to people ever really 
understanding any of this stuff. It is anti-science fiction.

Of course, the effects are nice. 

