Date: Sat, 20 Nov 1993 00:59:47 -0500
From: ddf2@postoffice.mail.cornell.edu (David Friedman)
Subject: Medieval Iceland was NOT a PPL!  (Discussion welcome)

>        Also, from the small amount of information I have gathered so
>far, I assert that the Icelandic system was NOT a "PPL".  There was only
>one source of law, the Allthing court.  The lesser Thing courts did not
>appear to be a source of law independently of the Allthing.  The system
>was definitely centralized.  I don't see how the system can be considered
>"polycentric" at all.

You are correct, and I think I made that clear in my chapter on Iceland.
Law enforcement was private, and the laws were made by a legislature seats
on which were effectively tradable private property (since they were part
of the godord, the transferable bundle of rights that defined a chieftain).
But there was only one legislature. The one hint of polycentric law occurs
briefly in the year 1000, during the conflict between Christian and Pagan
parties. The two sides declare themselves "out of law with each other." 
Cooler heads prevail, an arbitrator is chosen, and they end up again with a
single legal system.

The source of law was not, by the way, any of the courts at the Allthing
but the Logretta, which was the legislature, also at the Allthing, which
was an annual assempbly.

Iceland gives us an example of private, competing law enforcement, in a
system with a single legislature, a single court system, and no executive
arm of government.

