>From: Charlie Stross <charless@sco.com>
>Subject: How Voluntary Socialism is Disproven Ration
>
>you [starr@genie.slhs.udel.edu] write ...
>        :
>>Voluntary socialism is impossible because without the emergence of the
>>phenomena of prices from the exchange of private property rights, the
>>rational economic calculation we need in order to best allocate scarce
>>resources (means) to produce goods and services (ends) is impossible.
>
>Isn't this making a big assumption? Namely that resources are, and
>will remain, scarce?
>
>How un-extropian.
>
>(Clue  -- :-)

Cute, but it's worth noting that there's no need to allocate resources
that aren't scarce.

>Seriously, you make a very good case against economic socialism.  To
>undermine it would necessitate some fundamental changes: either to a
>society of plenty (where the available resources exceed everybody's
>combined maximum requirements), or to a redefinition of property or
>individuality.  If you subscribe to the idea of a singularity then any
>one of those three contingencies might come about, but there's no
>obvious way of predicting them.  Hmm.

I'm betting against any singularity.

>Can you make a case against all forms of non-economic socialism? (Such
>as families ...)
>
>I'd like to see such a proposal.
I can think of two lines of attack: commons, and transaction costs - but
in order to do it, you have to take into consideration why people form
these things, their costs and benefits.

Becker (Gary, Nobel economist, Chicago boy, former colleague of David
Friedman's) considers families firms, and I see no reason not to.  Coase
(Ronald, same description) speculates in his essay "The Theory of the
Firm" (published in a collection of his essays titled "The Firm, the Market,
and the Law") that firms arise in order to minimize "transaction costs,"
as Stigler (George, same description) dubbed them.  I hypothesize that
firms do this to the point at which the benefits from doing so equal the
cost of the commons problems of common ownership of firm property, and I'll
try to explain what I mean by all this.
Transaction costs seem to be things like the cost of searching for market
opportunities, the costs of governing a group of collaborators, the costs
of exchanging things between people, etc.  Coase speculates that firms
arise out of local economies between people who can reduce these costs,
through specialization, perhaps, in which a single entrepreneur head
a firm and does all the searching for opportunities to market the firms
output for the rest of the firm's workers, through purchasing input goods
in bulk at a discount rate then exchanging them for free within the firm
in order to produce output goods, etc.

Commons problems are basically that when no single individual owns and
bears the costs of getting/keeping something, people tend to use it up
without regard to saving it.  It occurs to me that all the instances of
voluntary socialism that I know of personally here in Berkeley are co-op
houses in which people live without maintaining them very much.  Houses
are fixed capital with very slow depreciation compared to many other
things, so this problem isn't very important in the short run; but it's
amusing to think of all the Greens I know who live in such unsustainable
living situations!

If I'm right about this - and I'm not sure what I really should do to prove
it - then there are two ways to go about getting rid of, or, rather, shrinking
the size of, firms: reducing transaction costs, or increasing the costs
of commons problems.  Both seem to be occurring in the US economy today.
The Information Technology Revolution is reducing transaction costs by
making vertical hierarchies for the transmission of information unnecessary,
and the American Empire is doing its damndest to increase the costs of
running a firm, whether commons problems costs or not.

Thus, a general tendency towards an independent contractor economy, which is
all to the good as far as I'm concerned.  Thats the theory.  Now check it
out with statistics to see how it applies.

>From: nancy@genie.slhs.udel.edu
>Subject:  Re:  How Voluntary Socialism is Disproven Rationally
>
>I'm not sure that Tim has disproven all possible forms of socialism
>by bringing up the calculation problem, since after the Singularity,
>people will be able to calculate much better.

Calculational ability doesn't affect the outcome of the argument,
because that isn't the problem.  The lack of input, what Tim May
calls "signaling" is the problem.  Suppose you need to install some
wiring in a building without prices.  Do you use, copper, silver, or
gold?  Assume differences in labor and conductivity to be too small
to make a difference in terms of the performance of the task you want
the wires to perform.  There's no way that even the most perfect
calculating agent could figure out the answer, because there's no
data, no input for the calculation.  Is copper "cheaper" than gold
without prices?  Nope.

>Also, would a society in which everyone voluntarily lives in communes
>that own property and compete/cooperate with each other count as
>free market or socialist? I think that a society like that would
>avoid the calculation problem, at least to a fairly large extent.

It would all be one big firm.  It would be fascist.  Since socialism's
impossible, fascism is the inevitable result of attempts to realize
socialism.  Perhaps "corporatist" would be a better term than "fascist."

Craig Presson's contribution:

